The maximum amount of methyl mercaptan that is stored at the Facility is 78,959 pounds. 
3. General Accidental Release Prevention Program and Specific Prevention Steps
The potential hazards that could lead to an accidental release of the methyl mercaptan include, but are not limited to, equipment failure, damage to piping, damage to equipment, human error, etc. Per the OSHA Process Safety Management (PSM) program that is part of the Risk Management Program, DRT has established numerous programs that serve to prevent accidental releases of methyl mercaptan. The programs include: 
	Process Safety Information; 
	Process Hazard Analysis; 
	Operating Procedures; 
	Training; 
	Mechanical Integrity; 
	Management of Change; 
	Pre Startup Safety Review; 
	Compliance Audits; 
	Incident Investigation; 
	Employee Participation; 
	Hot Work Permits; and 
	Contractor Safety. 
4. Five-year Accident History
There are no known releases of methyl mercaptan to have occurred at the DRT facility in the last five years.
5. Emergency Response Program.
As described in the Risk Management Program, DRT is not required to maintain an emergency response program (ERP). However, DRT participates in annual on-site exercises with local emergency response agencies and maintains an Emergency Preparedness and Response Plan. This plan and associated documents are maintained in the Facility Health and Safety Managers office.
6. Planned Changes to Improve Safety
Through our ongoing implementation of the Risk Management Program, DRT looks continuously for possible methyl mercaptan system changes to improve safety in our facility.
